WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.732
Merge and rebase both bring two lines of work back together.

2
00:00:06.732 --> 00:00:08.798
So what is the real difference?

3
00:00:08.798 --> 00:00:10.998
And why do people argue about it?

4
00:00:10.998 --> 00:00:11.997
Let's find out.

5
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:17.985
Picture Ana and Ben, who each take a copy of the shop's price list home for the weekend.

6
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.488
Ana groups the thousands with commas.

7
00:00:20.488 --> 00:00:22.779
Ben moves the currency to the front.

8
00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:25.769
And on Monday, both have changed the same line.

9
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:30.905
The easy way to combine them is to keep whichever copy arrived last.

10
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:34.543
And that is how a weekend of work quietly disappears.

11
00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:39.528
Last one wins is a fine rule for a race, and a terrible rule for a document.

12
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:44.964
So what we really want is both changes, and a question only when they truly clash.

13
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:49.208
In Git, each copy is a branch, a sticky note on a commit.

14
00:00:49.208 --> 00:00:51.496
Let's start with the easy shape.

15
00:00:51.496 --> 00:00:56.929
Main sits at d6aad6e, and a branch called other has one commit on top of it.

16
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.325
On main, you run git merge other.

17
00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.420
Pause and guess.

18
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.295
Does Git need to create anything new here?

19
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:05.473
No.

20
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:08.362
Main gained nothing since other split off.

21
00:01:08.362 --> 00:01:11.183
So Git just slides the main note forward.

22
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:12.696
That's a fast-forward.

23
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.170
Now the real shape.

24
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:21.712
Ana's commit on main and Ben's on currency-first both start from c711550.

25
00:01:21.712 --> 00:01:23.236
Both sides moved.

26
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.097
Before we merge, a puzzle.

27
00:01:26.097 --> 00:01:28.517
Say one line has two versions.

28
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:31.018
Main says the tax rate is 0.25.

29
00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:32.712
The branch says 0.20.

30
00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:35.293
Which one should the merge keep?

31
00:01:35.293 --> 00:01:36.584
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:40.397
Honestly, you can't tell.

33
00:01:40.397 --> 00:01:41.795
Maybe main raised it.

34
00:01:41.795 --> 00:01:43.659
Maybe the branch lowered it.

35
00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:47.187
Two versions are not enough to know who changed what.

36
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:52.232
So Git brings in a third version, the newest commit that both branches contain.

37
00:01:52.232 --> 00:01:56.200
In our story, that's Friday's copy, the one they both took home.

38
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.287
Say the base has 0.20.

39
00:01:58.287 --> 00:01:59.573
Now it's plain.

40
00:01:59.573 --> 00:02:02.575
The branch never touched that line.

41
00:02:02.575 --> 00:02:06.005
Only main changed it, so Git takes 0.25.

42
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.259
That third version is called the merge base.

43
00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:14.926
In our repo, git merge-base main currency-first prints c711550.

44
00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:20.481
Base plus two tips makes three, which is why it's called a three-way merge.

45
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.970
A change made on only one side goes in as it is.

46
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.787
Only a line that both sides changed, in different ways, is a conflict.

47
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:32.523
And Ana and Ben did change the same line.

48
00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:36.061
Run git merge currency-first, and Git stops halfway.

49
00:02:36.733 --> 00:02:40.442
The file now holds both versions, between marker lines.

50
00:02:40.442 --> 00:02:42.397
Your side, from HEAD, on top.

51
00:02:42.397 --> 00:02:44.689
Ben's side below the equals signs.

52
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:48.407
The first time, it looks as if Git broke your file.

53
00:02:48.407 --> 00:02:49.088
It didn't.

54
00:02:49.088 --> 00:02:50.927
It's asking you a question.

55
00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:53.039
Here both ideas are good.

56
00:02:53.039 --> 00:03:00.615
So you write one line with the currency first, and the commas kept, and you delete every marker.

57
00:03:00.615 --> 00:03:03.140
The program prints EUR 1,543.12.

58
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:04.639
One warning, though.

59
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:08.360
Git checks that the text merged, not that the code works.

60
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.841
So run the tests after every conflict.

61
00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:16.633
Then git add marks the file resolved, and git commit finishes the merge.

62
00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:19.423
Git writes a merge commit, with two parents.

63
00:03:19.423 --> 00:03:21.516
Main's old tip, and Ben's commit.

64
00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:27.757
In the office, it staples both drafts together, with a cover note that says, these two joined here.

65
00:03:27.757 --> 00:03:30.341
Nothing is lost, and nothing is rewritten.

66
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:33.658
Now rewind, and try the other way.

67
00:03:33.658 --> 00:03:36.188
On Ben's branch, run git rebase main.

68
00:03:36.188 --> 00:03:41.316
Rebase retypes Ben's pages, so they start after the latest version of main.

69
00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:43.697
Same words, fresh pages.

70
00:03:43.697 --> 00:03:45.183
Pause and guess.

71
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:49.551
Does Ben's retyped commit keep its ID, d229503?

72
00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:52.338
It can't.

73
00:03:52.338 --> 00:03:56.169
Remember from last time, a commit's ID covers its parent.

74
00:03:56.169 --> 00:04:01.076
The retyped commit has a new parent, so it's a new commit, with a new ID.

75
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:04.332
Then the currency-first note moves to the copy.

76
00:04:04.332 --> 00:04:08.572
The old commit still exists, but no branch points at it any more.

77
00:04:08.572 --> 00:04:10.268
The reflog still knows it.

78
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:15.231
The result is a straight line of history, as if Ben had started his work today.

79
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.680
Merge kept both drafts, stapled.

80
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.603
Rebase retyped one of them.

81
00:04:19.603 --> 00:04:22.524
Both end with Ana's and Ben's changes in.

82
00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:26.407
Now the rule that matters more than the rest.

83
00:04:26.407 --> 00:04:31.677
Say a teammate already copied Ben's branch, with the old d229503 in it.

84
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:34.233
Ben rebases and shares the new copy.

85
00:04:34.233 --> 00:04:37.783
Now the teammate holds pages the branch no longer has.

86
00:04:37.783 --> 00:04:43.961
If they merge, their history holds each of Ben's changes twice, under the old and the new IDs.

87
00:04:44.133 --> 00:04:46.830
Unlike a typist, Git retypes in a second.

88
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.184
That's exactly why this mistake is so easy to make.

89
00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:53.581
So rebase your own branch before anyone builds on it.

90
00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:56.768
Never rebase commits that other people already have.

91
00:04:56.933 --> 00:05:00.895
And after a rebase of your own branch, a normal push is refused.

92
00:05:00.895 --> 00:05:04.856
A plain force push overwrites the shared branch, no matter what.

93
00:05:04.856 --> 00:05:10.303
Force with lease refuses if a teammate pushed in the meantime, so their commit survives.

94
00:05:10.303 --> 00:05:11.851
Use the lease every time.

95
00:05:12.733 --> 00:05:14.585
So, the moral of the story.

96
00:05:14.585 --> 00:05:15.545
Merge staples.

97
00:05:15.545 --> 00:05:16.573
Rebase retypes.

98
00:05:16.573 --> 00:05:18.836
A fast-forward only moves a note.

99
00:05:18.836 --> 00:05:22.334
A three-way merge compares both tips with the base.

100
00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:26.448
And rebase makes new commits, so keep it to your own branch.

101
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.153
A quick check.

102
00:05:28.153 --> 00:05:30.943
Your teammate already pulled your branch.

103
00:05:30.943 --> 00:05:32.644
Should you rebase it now?

104
00:05:32.644 --> 00:05:33.733
Pause and guess.

105
00:05:35.933 --> 00:05:36.155
No.

106
00:05:36.155 --> 00:05:42.960
Their copy holds the old pages, so a rebase would leave every change in their history twice.

107
00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:43.996
Merge instead.
